<section>
  <h2>How the runs are made (input-driven version)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Start:</b> today’s GFS analysis winds at 850, 700, 500 and 200 hPa (observed state, no forecast steps), with the seedling, any Arabian Sea low and the south-west Bay circulation taken out; those two are put back as swirls that move along each member’s chosen path.</li>
    <li><b>Future winds = today’s winds changed by the inputs, step by step in time.</b> Each member sets: ridge position now (north–south, east–west), ridge drift (south / stays / north, 0.4° a day), ridge strength trend (easterlies −15% / steady / +15% over 3 days), the next westerly trough (when it reaches 80°E, how deep, how far south it digs; it moves east by itself), the MJO (fades and moves east / holds / strengthens over the Bay), the north-east monsoon easterlies, the 200 hPa westerly jet, the Arabian Sea low’s path, the south-west Bay circulation’s path, and storm size. No forecast model and no past-storm winds are used.</li>
    <li><b>51 combinations:</b> member 0 has every input as observed; members 1–50 follow an orthogonal design, so every setting of every input meets every setting of every other input. Formation time (12–54 h), start position (±1° north–south, ±1.5° east–west), sea temperature (observed ±0.2 °C) and shear are spread evenly over their ranges with a fixed Halton sequence (no random numbers).</li>
    <li><b>Track and strength:</b> our steering model (fitted on every Bay storm since 1980) moves the storm with these winds; strength from our fitted 12-hour change model, with inland decay over land.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section>
  <h2>How far to trust it</h2>
  <ul class="note">
    <li>This version cannot be tested on past storms the way the others were, because its inputs describe how the winds will change, and those changes are only known afterwards.</li>
    <li>With today’s winds, the storm sits well south of the ridge (about 19°N) in strong easterlies with a slight northerly component, so it moves west-south-west. Within these input ranges nothing turns it north: even a deep trough reaching 12°N, the ridge drifting north 1.2° a day, or the easterlies weakening by 70% still bring it to Tamil Nadu (only later).</li>
    <li>ECMWF and GFS turn this system north because they expect a bigger change than these offsets: the mid- and upper-level easterlies over the central Bay giving way to westerlies (the ridge breaking) within 2–3 days. That change is not one of the inputs here unless it is added as its own setting.</li>
    <li>Strength: treat as one category either way. Shares are of these 51 members, not calibrated probabilities.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
</div>
